<h2 id="recap">What you have now</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Structs hold named fields; values copy and <code>==</code> compares fields.</li>
  <li>Read-only by default; <code>ref this</code> marks changing methods, which need a <code>var</code>.</li>
  <li><code>with</code> makes a changed copy; <code>formatInto</code> controls printing, and <code>as: .debug</code> prints fields.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="quiz">Check your understanding</h2>
<example name="book-05-q1" output="no"/>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>What does this program print?</summary>
<pre><code>1 2 3</code></pre>
  <p><code>b</code> copies <code>a</code>, so <code>a.w</code> stays 1; <code>c</code> replaces <code>b.w</code> and <code>b</code> stays 2. <code>2 2 3</code> treats <code>b</code> as another name for <code>a</code>.</p>
</details>
<example name="book-05-q2" output="no"/>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>What does this program print?</summary>
<pre><code>5
Tally { n: 5 }</code></pre>
  <p><code>add()</code> uses the default 1, <code>add(amount: 4)</code> adds 4, so <code>n</code> is 5. The second line is the debug form; <code>Tally { n: 1 }</code> means only the first call ran.</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>A method does <code>count += 1</code> but its signature is <code>void add()</code>. What happens?</summary>
  <p>It is a compile error (<code>BYN2115</code>): the compiler points at the changing line and the help adds <code>ref this</code>. It does not quietly work on a copy.</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Why is <code>c.add()</code> an error after <code>let c = Counter {}</code>, with <code>add</code> a <code>ref this</code> method?</summary>
  <p>A <code>let</code> cannot change and <code>add</code> changes it (<code>BYN2104</code>). Declare <code>var c</code>: the method and call are right, the declaration is not.</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Why does <code>print($"{p}")</code> fail for a struct <code>Point</code>?</summary>
  <p>The compiler does not guess your type's shape (<code>BYN2225</code>). Add <code>formatInto(ref StrBuf)</code>, or print with <code>{p, as: .debug}</code>.</p>
</details>

